Write a prompt that recreates a Python CLI tool that scans text files for
non-English content, using pluggable language-detection backends, parallel
processing, and sanitized output.
Describe purpose, inputs/outputs, dependencies, logic, error handling, and edge
cases without code.
Create a self-contained prompt for an AI coding agent to build a Python 3
command-line utility named for detecting non-English text in text-based files.
Target environment: Termux on Android (shebang uses the Termux Python path), so
the script must run under standard CPython with the `from __future__ import
annotations` import and no platform-specific APIs. **Purpose** The tool
recursively scans files and directories given on the command line and reports
which files contain text written in languages other than English.
It should be usable as a quick auditing or linting utility for source trees,
documentation, or data dumps. **Command-line interface** - Use `argparse` with
the description "Detect non-English text in text-based files." - Positional
argument `paths`: zero or more paths, each converted to `pathlib.Path`.
When none are given, default to the current directory. - Option `-b` /
`--backend`: one of `pycld2`, `gcld3`, `langdetect`, `lingua`,
`fast_langdetect`, `fasttext`.
Default `pycld2`.
Help text: "Language-detection backend.
Defaults to pycld2." - Parse and return the namespace from a `parse_arguments`
function. **Constants to define at module level** - Worker process count: 8. -
Read chunk size: 64 KiB. - Detection sample size: 4000 characters. - Minimum
number of alphabetic letters required before attempting detection: 20. - A
module-level backend name string and a detector placeholder, both initialized
empty/None, to be set once per worker process. - A frozen set of directory names
to skip during traversal: version-control folders (.git, .hg, .svn, .bzr),
caches and tool folders (__pycache__, .mypy_cache, .pytest_cache, .ruff_cache,
.tox), virtual environments (.venv, venv, env, .env), node_modules, and IDE
folders (.idea, .vscode). **Data model** A frozen dataclass `ScanResult` with
fields: `path` (Path), `found` (bool, whether non-English text was detected),
`languages` (tuple of language code strings), and `error` (optional string).
**Core behavior** 1.
Expand the input paths: files are scanned directly; directories are walked
recursively, skipping any directory whose name is in the skip set.
Ignore symlinks or handle them safely to avoid cycles. 2.
Filter to text-based files.
Detect binary files (e.g., presence of null bytes or failed decoding) and skip
them.
Read files in fixed-size chunks. 3.
Decode content incrementally using an incremental UTF-8 decoder so multi-byte
characters split across chunk boundaries are handled correctly.
Tolerate invalid bytes gracefully (replace or ignore) rather than crashing. 4.
Take up to the detection sample size of decoded text.
If it contains fewer than the minimum number of alphabetic letters, report it as
not containing non-English text without invoking the detector. 5.
Invoke the selected backend's detector on the sample.
Map results to language codes.
A file is flagged `found` when any detected language is not English (code `en`).
Normalize codes to lowercase and ignore unreliable or unknown results. 6.
Process files in parallel using a process pool with 8 workers.
Each worker initializes its backend once (via a pool initializer that sets the
module-level backend name and loads the detector), so the detector is not
reloaded per file. 7.
Lazily import the backend library only when selected.
Backends and their pip packages: pycld2 (`pycld2`), gcld3 (`gcld3`), langdetect
(`langdetect`), lingua (`lingua-language-detector`), fast_langdetect
(`fast-langdetect`), fasttext (`fasttext` plus a downloaded language-ID model
such as `lid.176.ftz`).
If the required package is missing, raise a clear error explaining which package
to install. **Output** - Use `loguru`'s `logger` for all output and diagnostics
(not bare print for logs). - For each file flagged as containing non-English
text, log the path and the detected languages. - Log per-file errors (unreadable
files, permission errors, decode problems) as warnings or errors without
aborting the whole run. - At the end, log a summary: total files scanned and
count of files with non-English text. - Exit with status 0 when the scan
completes; use a non-zero status for fatal configuration errors such as an
unavailable backend. **Error handling and edge cases** - Nonexistent input
paths: log an error and continue with the remaining paths. - Empty files,
whitespace-only files, and files with fewer than 20 letters: skip detection, not
flagged. - Very large files: never load the whole file; only read up to the
chunk logic and the 4000-character sample. - Keyboard interrupt: shut down the
pool cleanly. - Worker exceptions: catch per-file so one failure does not stop
others. - Keep the script importable: put execution under a `main` guard, and
use `multiprocessing` safe patterns (a top-level worker function and picklable
arguments). **Execution** Run with `python <script> [paths...] [-b BACKEND]`.
Make the script executable with the Termux Python shebang line.
Ensure the code is single-file, uses type hints with postponed annotations, and
follows standard PEP 8 style with clear docstrings on public functions. ---
